            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has quite a bit of air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I also recommend replacing the weather stripping along both of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

There are two fresh air units located within the home, one in the kitchen, and other in the living room. These are designed to allow fresh air into the home. These should be opened on a needed basis, or if you notice some moisture on the windows. When closed, there is still quite a bit of air leakage coming from them. I recommend to replace the rubber gasket/seal to ensure a tight fit when closed. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $50 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.3 GJ. 

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $330 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $2,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 4.5 inches (R12) of blown fiberglass insulation. As you mentioned you may be interested in adding more insulation, I have modelled adding 11 inches (R38) of blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R50.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R50, you could potentially save $ XXX per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by XXX GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house appears to have 8 inches (R28) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 4 inches (R20) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation.



Savings:

By adding 4 inches (R20) of extruded polystyrene insulation to the cathedral ceiling, you could potentially save $260 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilingsCathedral>
                <foundation>

The floor above your crawl space appears to be 2x8 joists with 8 inches (R28) fiberglass batt insulation between the joists. 



Savings:

By adding 4 inches (R20) , of extruded polystyrene insulation to the underside of the floor, you could potentially save $160 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate 100 percent of the floor and increase its insulation value by a minimum of RSI 4.2 (R-24). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, they are however in reasonable shape, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the 6 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $230 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.1 GJ.  </windows>
                <doors>

I recommend replacing your front entry door (between the home and cold porch). I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified door rated for climate zone 3.



Savings:

By replacing your front door with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified door rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $90  per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 70 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous oil-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.68 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous oil-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.68, you could potentially save $850 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 82.8% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, which also incorporates an ECM fan blower motor. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented outside), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, that also incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $590 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM



On April 1, 2019, the AEA launched the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (Program), providing up to $20,000 to help owners of older, less efficient homes achieve significant gains in the energy efficiency and home comfort. Completing an exterior wall insulation upgrade is a required first step to participate in this Program, this must be a minimum increase from the current insulation value by R10. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, there are other rebates available for reducing air leakage, upgrading windows and heating systems, and a bonus for reducing total energy use by a minimum 20%.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



If you are interested in adding addition main wall exterior insulation and accessing the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, please do not hesitate to contact me. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Deline:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.39 per litre 

Wood - $472.5 per cord 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

